- What rollback strategy would you use if a tenant adapter regresses performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests production-grade RAG design across multilingual, heterogeneous corpora with multi-tenant isolation and performance constraints at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Hierarchical chunking (document → sections → paragraphs) with size optimization
- Multilingual embeddings and incremental refresh pipelines
- Vector DB partitioning with ACLs per tenant
- Two-stage retrieval with semantic search and cross-encoder reranking
- Freshness management, latency budgets (200-500ms), and comprehensive failover strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch for chunking and indexing
function chunkDocument(text, maxBytes = 4000) {
  const chunks = [];
  let current = '';

  // Split by paragraphs and respect semantic boundaries
  const paragraphs = text.split(/\n\s*\n/);

  for (const paragraph of paragraphs) {
    if (current.length + paragraph.length > maxBytes) {
      if (current) chunks.push(current.trim());
      current = paragraph;
    } else {
      current += (current ? '\n\n' : '') + paragraph;
    }
  }

  if (current) chunks.push(current.trim());
  return chunks;
}

// Tenant-aware retrieval
async function retrieveChunks(query, tenantId, limit = 5) {
  const embeddedQuery = await embedQuery(query);
  const results = await vectorStore.search({
    vector: embeddedQuery,
    filter: { tenantId },
    limit: limit * 2 // Get more for reranking
  });

  // Rerank with cross-encoder
  return await rerank(query, results).slice(0, limit);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This scenario tests the ability to design concrete evaluation frameworks for multi-source policy reasoning in regulated environments—where accuracy and traceability are legally required.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-source faithfulness: Ensuring the model attributes claims to correct sources
- Per-sentence provenance: Granular citation at the sentence level, not just document-level
- Conflict detection: Identifying contradictory information across internal policies and external regulations

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractCitations(answer, sources) {
  const sentences = answer.split(/[.!?]+/).filter(s => s.trim());
  return sentences.map(sentence => {
    const relevantSources = sources.filter(s => 
      sentence.toLowerCase().includes(s.keyphrase.toLowerCase())
    );
    return { sentence: sentence.trim(), citations: relevantSources };
  });
}

function detectConflicts(sources) {
  const conflicts = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < sources.length; i++) {
    for (let j = i + 1; j < sources.length; j++) {
      if (sources[i].contradicts?.(sources[j])) {
        conflicts.push({ sourceA: sources[i], sourceB: sources[j] });
      }
    }
  }
  return conflicts;
}

function generateAnswerWithCitations(query, policyDB, regulatoryDB) {
  const policyResults = policyDB.retrieve(query);
  const regulatoryResults = regulatoryDB.retrieve(query);
  const allSources = [...policyResults, ...regulatoryResults];

  const answer = generateAnswer(query, allSources);
  const citations = extractCitations(answer, allSources);
  const conflicts = detectConflicts(allSources);

  return { answer, citations, conflicts: conflicts.length > 0 ? conflicts : null };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Veeva needs AI systems that won't hallucinate medical information. This tests system design, ML evaluation, and understanding of production ML constraints.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates discuss: semantic similarity embeddings, factual consistency verification, confidence thresholds, human review workflows, and monitoring false positives/negatives.

## Code Example
```typescript
class HallucinationDetector {
  async evaluate(response: string, sources: string[]) {
    const semanticScore = await this.computeSimilarity(response, sources);
    const factualScore = await this.verifyFacts(response);
    const confidence = this.calculateConfidence(semanticScore, factualScore);
    return { isHallucinated: confidence < 0.95, confidence };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle edge cases where sources conflict?
- What metrics would you track to monitor system performance?
- How would you optimize for latency while maintaining accuracy?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical, end-to-end RAG pipeline with real-world constraints: chunking, embedding stability, vector DB tenancy, latency targets, and robust failover. It also probes how to evaluate recall/latency at scale across multiple tenants.

## Key Concepts

- Chunking strategy: 512-token blocks aligned to maintain context.
- Embeddings: stable models (SBERT or OpenAI Ada) with versioned updates.
- Vector DB tenancy: per-repo namespaces and strict access controls.
- Freshness: index aging, re-embedding on document updates.
- Latency: retrieval latency targets (p95/p50) and caching.
- Failover: lexical BM25 fallback and provenance surface.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: split text into 512-token chunks using a simple tokenizer (word-based placeholder)
function chunkDoc(text) {
  const tokens = text.split(/\s+/);
  const chunkSize = 512;
  const chunks = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tokens.length; i += chunkSize) {
    chunks.push(tokens.slice(i, i + chunkSize).join(' '));
  }
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure drift in embeddings over time and trigger re-embedding schedules?
- What tenancy/ACL considerations would you enforce for cross-repo queries?
- How would you structure A/B tests for recall vs latency trade-offs?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to craft brand-isolated PEFT strategies with governance for multilingual, cross-brand assistants.

## Key Concepts
- PEFT, LoRA, QLoRA; per-brand adapters; versioned KB drift; privacy and data governance; latency.

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo-injection outline for adapters
function injectAdapters(model, brand){
  model.addAdapter('attn', {rank:8, alpha:32, type:'LoRA', quant:4});
  model.addAdapter('ffn', {rank:8, alpha:32, type:'LoRA', quant:4});
  model.selectBrand(brand);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor drift and roll back adapters? 
- How would you audit data flows per brand?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a candidate’s ability to design end-to-end evaluation for private, privacy-conscious enterprise assistants, focusing on faithfulness, hallucination, and safety under real-world data/versioning constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Grounding to versioned sources and source IDs
- Hallucination detection in live responses
- Privacy safety: PII/secret leakage checks in output and citations
- Performance metrics under load and latency budgets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal evaluation script outline (pseudo)
function evaluate(model, corpus) {
  const prompts = loadPrompts();
  return prompts.map(p => {
    const out = model.respond(p);
    const grounded = ground(out, corpus, p);
    return { prompt: p, output: out, grounded };
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect partial grounding failures (e.g., only some claims anchored)?
- How would you integrate this into a CI/CD pipeline with private data access controls?
